{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent upward bias needed: model forecasts are systematically too low versus outcomes during uptrends/rallies (typical underprediction ~+1 to +3 EUR, occasionally more). Apply a positive level shift when recent momentum is positive.",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: after a breakout (multi-day rise), the model reacts too slowly and stays anchored to the prior range. Add a momentum term: if last 5–10 days slope is positive, lift the entire 1–20d horizon, with the largest uplift in days 3–15.",
    "Underestimation of volatility on the upside: peaks are consistently missed (true series reaches ~69–70 while forecasts cap ~66–68). Inflate upside tails: widen forecast distribution and raise high-quantile path when recent realized vol increases.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: near-term (1–5d) errors are smaller; mid-horizon (5–20d) underprediction is largest during rallies. Use a ramping correction that increases from day 1 to ~day 10, then tapers.",
    "Over-mean-reversion at longer horizons: forecasts often drift back down (or flatten) while the true path stays elevated. Reduce mean-reversion strength when price is above its 20–30d moving average and MA is rising.",
    "Post-spike handling: when the model projects a pullback after a spike, it tends to pull back too early/too much; true outcomes often consolidate at higher levels. Replace sharp pullbacks with higher plateaus unless momentum turns negative."
  ]
}
